The existing solver already passes. One easy speedup is to compute the parameter term `su_p_p0 @ p0` once per solve instead of every iteration, using `solution.x` as scratch space until the end:
`src/fom_solver.c` passes `make verify`. The worst error across 8 samples was 6.2e-15, well under the 1e-6 tolerance.

A working solver was already in the file and passed verify as I found it. I made one speed change: the parameter term `su_p_p0 @ p0` doesn't change between iterations, so it is now computed once per solve. It's stored temporarily in `solution.x` and copied in as the starting value of `new_state` each iteration. That gets rid of one sparse matrix-vector product per iteration and adds no new buffers. `make bench` gives about 64 µs per solve on average and 57 µs at best. I didn't time the old version, so I don't know exactly how much this saves.

The solver meets the requirements:
- **Data and helpers:** every coefficient comes from `fom_data.c`, all products use the `fom_apply_*` helpers, and the schedule value is read once per iteration through `fom_sched_xi(it)`.
- **Embedded constraints:** there is no dynamic memory, no variable-length arrays and no `/` operator anywhere.
- **Reset:** `fom_setup` and `fom_update_params` both reset the state to `FOM_STATE_INIT`.
- **Iterations and residual:** with no tolerance set, it always runs `max_iter` iterations. The residual (largest absolute change in the state) is only computed on the last iteration.

There's no linear solve here, because this method has no steps that need one.
